course: dictionary
lesson: 12070
title: One word, many meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words have more than one meaning, and the context tells you which one the writer means. The word clearance is a good example. Its meanings are different, so you must read the words around it to decide. «The sign said: height clearance 3.2 metres.» The words height and sign tell you clearance means the space under something, here a bridge.
explain: «The shop had a clearance sale, with every coat half price.» The words sale and half price show that clearance means selling old stock cheaply. «She needed security clearance before she could enter the building.» Here clearance means official permission. Ask yourself: what is the topic of the sentence? That often tells you which meaning fits.
explain: Always check your meaning. Put it into the sentence in place of the word. «The sign said: the space under the bridge is 3.2 metres.» Does it still make sense? If yes, you have probably found the right meaning. If not, try another meaning from the list.
fact: Clearance is a noun. It can be uncountable, as in «clearance of land», or countable, as in «a clearance sale».
fact: Clearance can mean the act of clearing something away, such as clearing land or rubbish.
fact: Clearance can mean official permission, as in «security clearance».
fact: Clearance can mean the space between two things, such as the height under a bridge.
fact: In football and rugby, a clearance is kicking or heading the ball away from your own goal.
exercise: «The sign on the bridge said: height clearance 3.2 metres.» What does clearance mean here? | A sale of old goods at low prices | Official permission to build a bridge | *The space under the bridge, so a lorry of this height can pass | Kicking a ball away from a goal
exercise: «The shop had a clearance sale, so every coat was half price.» What is a clearance sale for? | *To sell old stock at low prices | To give free coats to children | To ask people for permission to shop | To clear the rubbish from the street
exercise: Lena says, «The workers needed security clearance before they could enter the building.» What does clearance mean here? | A height limit for a door | A cheap sale of old chairs | The act of tidying a room | *Official permission to go in
test: «The workers began the clearance of the old building site, taking away rubble and rubbish.» What does clearance mean here? | Official permission to enter the site | The space between two walls | *The act of clearing things away | A low price sale of building tools
test: Why would a driver look carefully at a height clearance sign? | To find the cheapest petrol station | *To check how high their vehicle can be to pass under a bridge | To count how many cars are on the bridge | To ask for security clearance
exam: «The clearance under the railway bridge is only 2.5 metres, so the tall lorry could not pass.» What does clearance mean here? | *The space between the road and the bridge | A sale of cheap lorries | Permission to drive under the bridge | Kicking the lorry away from the bridge
